      "math_academy_id": "e-5876",
      "problem": "What is the domain of $f(x) = \\sqrt{3x+5}-2?$",
      "worked_solution": "The domain of $y = \\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $g(x) = \\sqrt{3x+5} -2,$ we replace $x$ with $3x+5$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n{3x+5} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+5 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -5 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{5}{3}, \\infty\\right).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14239347/assets/6f94991ce01ad98ec5bdffbb0673182dc40bc264803b6eea5154241985359c0b.png)",
